    fullDescription: "Dubai's flight-price pattern is the reverse of most European sun destinations, and understanding it saves real money. Because Dubai's peak tourist season is the cool winter — roughly November to March, when the weather is glorious — that's when demand and fares are highest, culminating in a sharp spike around Christmas and New Year, when the city throws its famous fireworks and hotels and flights are at their dearest. Conversely, the cheapest time to fly is the summer, June to August, which is Dubai's low season precisely because daytime temperatures routinely top 40°C and humidity soars, deterring many visitors; round-trip fares can drop to around 430 dollars, and hotels slash their rates too, so budget travellers who don't mind the heat (and who plan to spend much of the day in air-conditioned malls, indoor attractions and hotel pools) can find excellent value. The shoulder months of April, May, September and October offer a middle ground of moderate prices and hot-but-bearable weather. Whenever you go, the usual tactics apply: book roughly six to eight weeks ahead for the sweet spot on price, fly midweek rather than at weekends, stay flexible with your dates and use fare alerts to catch drops. As a major global hub served by Emirates, flydubai and dozens of other airlines, Dubai enjoys huge flight capacity and strong competition, which helps keep fares reasonable year-round compared with less-connected destinations.",
