    fullDescription: "Sightseeing costs in Dubai vary enormously, from completely free to eye-watering, so it pays to know the going rates and to book the big attractions ahead. Among the paid highlights: the Burj Khalifa's At The Top observation deck runs around 46 dollars (much more for the higher SKY level), the Museum of the Future about 39 dollars, the Dubai Frame around 14, Ain Dubai (the giant observation wheel) about 35, and the Dubai Aquarium combo around 44. Experiences cost more: a desert safari ranges from roughly 55 to 135 dollars depending on quality, the major water parks (Aquaventure, Wild Wadi) are around 74 to 95 dollars, and theme parks like IMG Worlds or Legoland run 80 to 95. Ski Dubai's Snow Park is about 68. Crucially, the timed-entry big-hitters — the Burj Khalifa and especially the Museum of the Future — regularly sell out days or weeks ahead, so book online as soon as your dates are set, both to secure a slot and often to save versus the gate price. Against all this, Dubai offers a remarkable amount for free: the Gold and Spice Souks, all the public beaches (JBR, Kite, Jumeirah, Sunset), the nightly Dubai Fountain shows, the Al Fahidi historic district, Dubai Marina and The Walk, and the 1-dirham abra ride cost nothing or next to nothing. A sensible approach is to pick two or three paid attractions you really want, book them ahead, and fill the rest of your trip with the city's abundant free sights and experiences to keep the budget in check.",
